      {
        "narration": "Cartographers marked a massive landmass known as Sandy Island in the Coral Sea between Australia and New Caledonia, even though solid earth wasn't actually there.",
        "visual_query": "coral sea ocean waves",
        "visual_fallback": "open ocean water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The whaling ship Velocity reported the island in 1876. For more than a century, it was included on official maps around the world.",
        "visual_query": "vintage nautical map compass",
        "visual_fallback": "old paper map"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Mapmakers and digital services like National Geographic and Google Maps dutifully included the phantom territory for years. But nobody actually bothered to verify it in person.",
        "visual_query": "digital satellite earth view",
        "visual_fallback": "globe map closeup"
      },
      {
        "narration": "That changed in 2012, when a team of Australian scientists sailed straight to the exact coordinates looking for geological anomalies. Instead of sandy beaches and palm trees, they found open ocean two miles deep.",
        "visual_query": "research vessel ocean horizon",
        "visual_fallback": "ship deck waves"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The mystery was finally solved when geologists realized whalers in the nineteenth century had likely mischarted a massive floating raft of pumice stone thrown up by a sudden underwater volcano. How does an entire mountain of volcanic glass fool the British Navy for generations?",
        "visual_query": "underwater volcanic rock formation",
        "visual_fallback": "dark ocean depths"
      }
    ],
